# Simplification

**Simplification** is the replacement of a complex problem or representation
by a less complex approximation or formulation. The result is easier to work
with and is no longer exactly the original. That deliberate loss of accuracy
is what separates it from [[abstraction]] and from [[summarization]], the two
operations it was named alongside.

## Description

| | Input | Output |
|---|---|---|
| Simplification | complex problem or representation | less complex approximation or formulation |

The question that opened this part of the discussion begins with the word:
"simplification.. abstract thinking and generating abstract summary for the
paper is not the same thing". It lists three things that are easily run
together, and the reply gives each its own line with an example.

| Operation | Example | What is given up |
|---|---|---|
| Simplification | point-mass approximation of a physical system | accuracy |
| Summarization | a shorter abstract of a technical paper | length and detail |
| Abstraction | group structure across permutations and rotations | nothing true of the structure |
| Idealization | frictionless planes, ideal gases | a factor known to be present |

Treating a planet as a point mass is a simplification. The planet is not a
point, and the model says so only by working well enough. An abstraction makes
no such trade: what is said about a group is exactly true of every instance. A
summary shortens a text and tries to keep what matters in it. It is about the
text, where a simplification is about the thing.

[[idealization]] stands next to simplification in the same table. The
discussion does not separate them further. On the examples given, an
idealization removes one named factor entirely, where a simplification
replaces the whole description by a cruder one.

A simplification has a range in which it holds, and outside it fails. So its
output should come with the conditions under which it was acceptable, which
is the kind of evidence a [[typed-transformation]] is meant to carry.

## Relations

- It is one [[cognitive-operation]] {relation: instance-of}: a complex representation in, a less complex approximation out.
- It is not [[abstraction]] {relation: distinguished-from}: an approximation, against an exact structure.
- It is not [[summarization]] {relation: distinguished-from}: a cruder model of the thing, against a shorter account of a text.
- It stands beside [[idealization]] {relation: distinguished-from}, which removes one factor outright.
- Whether a simplification is good enough is a matter for [[evaluation]] {relation: uses}.
- As an agent's operation it should be a [[typed-transformation]] {relation: is-a} that states where the approximation holds.
- It bears on the question [[learning-the-formalism]] {relation: answers}, as one of the operations mapped there.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[abstraction]].** Identifying structure independent of incidental detail.
  It loses detail without losing truth, which a simplification does not
  promise.
- **[[summarization]].** Condensing content while keeping what matters for a
  purpose. It shortens the telling and leaves the thing told alone.
- **[[evaluation]].** Assessing a claim against a criterion. For a
  simplification the criterion is whether the lost accuracy matters for the
  use.

## Queries

The relations declared on this card, as edges:

```hql
@[[simplification]] | uplinks | map(e => {to: e.target, how: e.data})
```

The cards that point here:

```hql
@[[simplification]] | downlinks | map(e => e.source)
```

